1766
01:16:53.055 --> 01:16:55.888
of unworthiness to make me work for something.

1767
01:16:55.888 --> 01:17:00.569
But those dreams are not necessarily to be fulfilled.

1768
01:17:00.569 --> 01:17:03.530
They are a vehicle that gets you moving.

1769
01:17:03.530 --> 01:17:06.822
There is something so much better in store for you

1770
01:17:06.822 --> 01:17:07.734
than that dream.

1771
01:17:07.734 --> 01:17:09.101
There's something bigger.

1772
01:17:09.101 --> 01:17:14.062
Because what happened to me is, by not making that dream,

1773
01:17:14.062 --> 01:17:16.663
I've learned an entirely new business model.

1774
01:17:16.663 --> 01:17:18.810
I mean, I have a huge partnership with Audible now.

1775
01:17:18.810 --> 01:17:20.868
Right, you've done a ton of stuff with Audible.

1776
01:17:20.868 --> 01:17:23.271
You're one of their leading partners in the whole

1777
01:17:23.271 --> 01:17:25.303
sort of listening space all together.

1778
01:17:25.303 --> 01:17:29.306
Yap, I never would have known that had I achieved my dream

1779
01:17:29.306 --> 01:17:31.470
of being a New York Times bestselling author.

1780
01:17:31.470 --> 01:17:34.037
If I had had a successful traditional book launch,

1781
01:17:34.037 --> 01:17:36.285
I never would be where I am right now.

1782
01:17:36.285 --> 01:17:37.196
Yeah, there's no way.

1783
01:17:37.196 --> 01:17:38.029
No way.

1784
01:17:38.029 --> 01:17:39.483
It would have satisfied your ego temporarily.

1785
01:17:39.483 --> 01:17:40.316
Correct.

1786
01:17:40.316 --> 01:17:42.779
For the two weeks or whatever that it was on the list.

1787
01:17:42.779 --> 01:17:46.461
And then after that, although all the rest, first of all,

1788
01:17:46.461 --> 01:17:47.968
you wouldn't have sold that many audio books

1789
01:17:47.968 --> 01:17:49.579
because the book would have been available.

1790
01:17:49.579 --> 01:17:50.412
Yap.

1791
01:17:50.412 --> 01:17:53.454
And you definitely would not have made the cake

1792
01:17:53.454 --> 01:17:54.699
that would have all gone to the publisher.

1793
01:17:54.699 --> 01:17:55.532
Yeah.

1794
01:17:55.532 --> 01:17:58.211
Which is insane, because I'm sure you just printed money

1795
01:17:58.211 --> 01:17:59.414
off of the audio book.

1796
01:17:59.414 --> 01:18:00.247
Yeah.

1797
01:18:00.247 --> 01:18:02.576
So self-publishing looking pretty good now.

1798
01:18:02.576 --> 01:18:04.942
Oh, extraordinarily great.
Unbelievable.

1799
01:18:04.942 --> 01:18:07.549
But so ,here's the other thing that that taught me.

1800
01:18:07.549 --> 01:18:10.327
It's what gave me that sort of high-five attitude,

1801
01:18:10.327 --> 01:18:11.430
this mantra.

1802
01:18:11.430 --> 01:18:14.708
And the mantra, because as I was schlepping through airports

1803
01:18:14.708 --> 01:18:17.481
and the book is out of stock and Tony Robbins' face

1804
01:18:17.481 --> 01:18:20.366
is everywhere, and I'm thinking I'm the world's biggest

1805
01:18:20.366 --> 01:18:24.495
failure, I kept saying this to myself on repeat.

1806
01:18:24.495 --> 01:18:27.666
"There is no way, Mel, if you've worked this hard

1807
01:18:27.666 --> 01:18:30.049
that you will not be rewarded.

1808
01:18:30.049 --> 01:18:34.166
You have to believe that this moment is preparing you

1809
01:18:34.166 --> 01:18:36.318
for something amazing that hasn't happened yet.

1810
01:18:36.318 --> 01:18:37.637
Keep going.

1811
01:18:37.637 --> 01:18:40.731
You have to believe that this moment is preparing you

1812
01:18:40.731 --> 01:18:42.923
for something amazing that hasn't happened yet,

1813
01:18:42.923 --> 01:18:44.380
keep going."

1814
01:18:44.380 --> 01:18:49.055
And so I repeated that over and over and over and over

1815
01:18:49.055 --> 01:18:52.889
and over again, as I wanted to throw in the towel,

1816
01:18:52.889 --> 01:18:55.352
as I would start to bash myself,

1817
01:18:55.352 --> 01:18:57.646
as I would start to feel sorry for myself and be like,

1818
01:18:57.646 --> 01:19:00.174
"Nope, there's just no way I'm gonna believe that something

1819
01:19:00.174 --> 01:19:01.328
amazing isn't gonna happen.

1820
01:19:01.328 --> 01:19:02.814
I've worked too hard.

1821
01:19:02.814 --> 01:19:06.472
Something amazing that hasn't happened is coming."

1822
01:19:06.472 --> 01:19:09.488
And when you get yourself into that mindset,

1823
01:19:09.488 --> 01:19:14.488
it creates a sense of resilience and momentum and resolve

1824
01:19:14.509 --> 01:19:17.943
that you need in order to keep going when the shit

1825
01:19:17.943 --> 01:19:20.428
hits the fan or when you feel disappointed or when life

1826
01:19:20.428 --> 01:19:22.337
is beating you down.

1827
01:19:22.337 --> 01:19:25.029
And that was the other gift of that moment,

1828
01:19:25.029 --> 01:19:30.029
is developing a little tool to flip my mindset when I wanted

1829
01:19:30.345 --> 01:19:33.401
to start to feel sorry for myself.

1830
01:19:33.401 --> 01:19:35.558
Throw the timeline out.

1831
01:19:35.558 --> 01:19:37.851
Divorce yourself from expectations.

1832
01:19:37.851 --> 01:19:40.417
Double down on that belief.

1833
01:19:40.417 --> 01:19:44.937
And, maintain your like adherence to just moving forward

1834
01:19:44.937 --> 01:19:46.221
and action.

1835
01:19:46.221 --> 01:19:47.835
Yeah.

1836
01:19:47.835 --> 01:19:51.241
So much easier to say than to do.

1837
01:19:51.241 --> 01:19:54.191
That's why I focus on tools, because we all know

1838
01:19:54.191 --> 01:19:55.399
what to do.

1839
01:19:55.399 --> 01:19:56.314
It's the how.

1840
01:19:56.314 --> 01:19:57.302
Yeah.

1841
01:19:57.302 --> 01:20:01.275
I think when we start to talk about the self,

1842
01:20:01.275 --> 01:20:05.544
the quote, unquote like self-help or self-improvement space,

1843
01:20:05.544 --> 01:20:08.382
you start to hear words like, you know, motivation

1844
01:20:08.382 --> 01:20:11.495
and inspiration and how do I find my purpose?

1845
01:20:11.495 --> 01:20:14.191
And, we need to be living with passion.

1846
01:20:14.191 --> 01:20:17.191
And, I just think for the most part,

1847
01:20:19.227 --> 01:20:22.467
it does more harm than good in the sense that,

1848
01:20:22.467 --> 01:20:24.286
it confuses people.

1849
01:20:24.286 --> 01:20:27.983
And I think it makes people feel bad about where they are

1850
01:20:27.983 --> 01:20:29.733
in the moment.

1851
01:20:29.733 --> 01:20:33.373
Because it often lacks any kind of practicality.

1852
01:20:33.373 --> 01:20:35.443
Like it's sort of a measuring tool.

1853
01:20:35.443 --> 01:20:38.362
Like, "Oh, I don't have passion or I don't know what

1854
01:20:38.362 --> 01:20:39.453
my purpose is.

1855
01:20:39.453 --> 01:20:42.443
Like I must not have self-worth that all these other people

1856
01:20:42.443 --> 01:20:46.127
seem all fired up and excited and here I am little me

1857
01:20:46.127 --> 01:20:47.403
over here and I'm like."

1858
01:20:47.403 --> 01:20:49.401
You become a shrinking flower.

1859
01:20:49.401 --> 01:20:52.772
And so I think the vernacular around it, like, I want you,

1860
01:20:52.772 --> 01:20:55.672
I'm saying this because I want you to help me make sense

1861
01:20:55.672 --> 01:20:58.599
of it for the person who's listening or watching

1862
01:20:58.599 --> 01:21:02.246
who is in that place, who's struggling with the idea

1863
01:21:02.246 --> 01:21:07.063
of how to move forward and how to make sense of the idea

1864
01:21:07.063 --> 01:21:10.537
of self-empowerment and self-improvement.

1865
01:21:10.537 --> 01:21:11.495
Yeah.

1866
01:21:11.495 --> 01:21:13.451
So that brings us to "The High Five Habit,"

1867
01:21:13.451 --> 01:21:18.201
which is gonna sound so stupid. (laughs)

1868
01:21:19.301 --> 01:21:20.874
More stupid.

1869
01:21:20.874 --> 01:21:23.123
You know, this is my brand I've come to realize.

1870
01:21:23.123 --> 01:21:23.956
I know, yeah.

1871
01:21:23.956 --> 01:21:25.863
If it sounds stupid and it's backed by science,

1872
01:21:25.863 --> 01:21:29.516
it's something Mel Robbins wants to talk about.

1873
01:21:29.516 --> 01:21:33.801
So "The High Five Habit" is for all of us.

1874
01:21:33.801 --> 01:21:37.220
But particularly if you're somebody that feels stuck

1875
01:21:37.220 --> 01:21:40.789
or you're easily tripped up or brought down by jealousy

1876
01:21:40.789 --> 01:21:44.937
or guilt or procrastination or beating yourself up

1877
01:21:44.937 --> 01:21:45.770
or comparison.

1878
01:21:45.770 --> 01:21:48.983
And you know, honestly, if you're breathing, that's you.

1879
01:21:48.983 --> 01:21:50.736
I mean, we all, who isn't?

1880
01:21:50.736 --> 01:21:51.569
Yeah.

1881
01:21:52.819 --> 01:21:56.819
This is an idea that once I unpack it for you,

1882
01:21:59.066 --> 01:22:02.706
you're gonna literally go, "Holy shit."

1883
01:22:02.706 --> 01:22:03.623
In a good way?

1884
01:22:03.623 --> 01:22:04.873
Yes.

1885
01:22:04.873 --> 01:22:07.033
All right, lay it on me.

1886
01:22:07.033 --> 01:22:11.616
So we all know that we need to love ourselves, right?

1887
01:22:13.178 --> 01:22:15.049
How do you do that?

1888
01:22:15.049 --> 01:22:16.591
I don't know.

1889
01:22:16.591 --> 01:22:18.094
What is the action that you take?

1890
01:22:18.094 --> 01:22:21.798
Yeah, we all know we're supposed to accept ourselves.

1891
01:22:21.798 --> 01:22:25.826
We all know we're supposed to have self-esteem

1892
01:22:25.826 --> 01:22:29.566
and self-worth and self-validation and self-confidence.

1893
01:22:29.566 --> 01:22:31.233
How do you build it?

1894
01:22:32.867 --> 01:22:36.366
And, you know, the other thing is that we also are looking

1895
01:22:36.366 --> 01:22:41.366
outside ourselves for confirmation, validation, affirmation

1896
01:22:41.569 --> 01:22:43.598
of those things.

1897
01:22:43.598 --> 01:22:46.373
And so how do you relocate the source

1898
01:22:46.373 --> 01:22:49.012
of your self-confidence, self-esteem, self-worth,

1899
01:22:49.012 --> 01:22:51.139
self-validation, within yourself so that you're in control

1900
01:22:51.139 --> 01:22:52.426
of it.

1901
01:22:52.426 --> 01:22:53.759
Big idea, right?

1902
01:22:55.213 --> 01:22:58.134
So, let me tell you the story about how I discovered this

1903
01:22:58.134 --> 01:23:00.679
because it was not like a, "Oh, how do I have another

1904
01:23:00.679 --> 01:23:03.550
high five moment, you know?"

1905
01:23:03.550 --> 01:23:05.738
I get fired from my talk show.

1906
01:23:05.738 --> 01:23:09.488
And I am essentially fired from my dream job.

1907
01:23:10.668 --> 01:23:13.486
I then start having speech after speech after speech

1908
01:23:13.486 --> 01:23:15.030
after speech cancel.

1909
01:23:15.030 --> 01:23:19.294
Then Houghton Mifflin cancels my contract for my next book

1910
01:23:19.294 --> 01:23:23.832
and asked for me to return money that I've long spent and.

1911
01:23:23.832 --> 01:23:24.864
Why did they cancel it?

1912
01:23:24.864 --> 01:23:25.697
Because.

1913
01:23:25.697 --> 01:23:26.577
Just because of pandemic related.

1914
01:23:26.577 --> 01:23:27.796
They laid off, there are two reasons.

1915
01:23:27.796 --> 01:23:30.627
First of all, they were laying off 45% of the staff,

1916
01:23:30.627 --> 01:23:33.004
but more importantly, let me take responsibility.

1917
01:23:33.004 --> 01:23:34.753
I was a year late on delivering a manuscript.

1918
01:23:34.753 --> 01:23:35.586
Okay.

1919
01:23:35.586 --> 01:23:36.419
So let's be honest.

1920
01:23:36.419 --> 01:23:37.502
I fucked up yet again.

1921
01:23:37.502 --> 01:23:40.908
I ghosted my publisher and they fired my ass,

1922
01:23:40.908 --> 01:23:41.812
as they should.

1923
01:23:41.812 --> 01:23:44.177
A long play to ghost your publisher, right?

1924
01:23:44.177 --> 01:23:45.430
(Mel laughing)

1925
01:23:45.430 --> 01:23:48.205
How about spend your money and then ghost you?

1926
01:23:48.205 --> 01:23:52.400
But I found myself again in this like familiar place

1927
01:23:52.400 --> 01:23:56.460
having sort of echoes back to a decade ago of,

1928
01:23:56.460 --> 01:23:59.741
"Oh my God, are we in financial free fall again?

1929
01:23:59.741 --> 01:24:02.404
Like, is this ride over?"

1930
01:24:02.404 --> 01:24:03.267
Kids come home.

1931
01:24:03.267 --> 01:24:05.926
When you did like 100 speeches in a year prior to that

1932
01:24:05.926 --> 01:24:07.161
or prior to the show.

1933
01:24:07.161 --> 01:24:07.994
Yap.

1934
01:24:07.994 --> 01:24:09.409
And then took a pay cut to do this show.

1935
01:24:09.409 --> 01:24:10.367
Yap.

1936
01:24:10.367 --> 01:24:12.193
And look, I've been saving money.

1937
01:24:12.193 --> 01:24:14.970
I've been very smart about money now that I've made it.

1938
01:24:14.970 --> 01:24:16.927
We've paid back all of our debt, something we're both

1939
01:24:16.927 --> 01:24:18.927
really, really proud of.

1940
01:24:19.965 --> 01:24:23.697
And at the same time, when the hits the fan in your life,

1941
01:24:23.697 --> 01:24:25.024
your old things will get triggered.

1942
01:24:25.024 --> 01:24:27.267
And what got triggered for me is, I'm about to lose it all

1943
01:24:27.267 --> 01:24:28.100
again.

1944
01:24:28.100 --> 01:24:28.933
Right.

1945
01:24:28.933 --> 01:24:30.354
Meanwhile, just like everybody else on the planet,

1946
01:24:30.354 --> 01:24:33.924
your kids come home and it's both a blessing and a horror

1947
01:24:33.924 --> 01:24:36.864
show to have your children in a state of distress

1948
01:24:36.864 --> 01:24:38.528
as the world is in distress.

1949
01:24:38.528 --> 01:24:40.571
And I just started to feel overwhelmed.

1950
01:24:40.571 --> 01:24:44.321
And there was this one morning where I walked

1951
01:24:46.957 --> 01:24:50.289
into the bathroom and I was standing in my underwear,

1952
01:24:50.289 --> 01:24:52.457
brushing my teeth in front of the mirror.

1953
01:24:52.457 --> 01:24:55.839
And I looked up at the mirror and my first thought was,

1954
01:24:55.839 --> 01:24:57.418
"Ugh."

1955
01:24:57.418 --> 01:25:01.218
I noticed that my jowls were starting to look like

1956
01:25:01.218 --> 01:25:04.999
saddlebags on a pack horse at the grand canyon.

1957
01:25:04.999 --> 01:25:09.666
And, I had like these crazy lines by my eyes and my neck

1958
01:25:10.977 --> 01:25:15.977
was really like kind of saggy and one boob was hanging lower

1959
01:25:16.273 --> 01:25:19.751
than the other and my gray hair was coming in.

1960
01:25:19.751 --> 01:25:24.064
And, as soon as I started kind of critiquing my thoughts,

1961
01:25:24.064 --> 01:25:28.163
my looks and appearance, then my mind, Rich, started going,

1962
01:25:28.163 --> 01:25:30.494
"Fuck, I didn't get that email back to that person

1963
01:25:30.494 --> 01:25:33.314
and I got that presentation I need to do.

1964
01:25:33.314 --> 01:25:35.722
And my God, did that speech just cancel again?

1965
01:25:35.722 --> 01:25:36.589
What the fuck am I gonna do?"

1966
01:25:36.589 --> 01:25:38.754
And then I look down and the dog needs to be walked.

1967
01:25:38.754 --> 01:25:40.874
And then I think, I got a zoom call in nine minutes.

1968
01:25:40.874 --> 01:25:42.610
Like I gotta get my together.

1969
01:25:42.610 --> 01:25:46.886
And before I knew it, my whole mood was low.

1970
01:25:46.886 --> 01:25:48.095
I felt overwhelmed.

1971
01:25:48.095 --> 01:25:50.540
I'd taken myself down mentally.

1972
01:25:50.540 --> 01:25:53.103
I just wanted somebody to walk in and be like,

1973
01:25:53.103 --> 01:25:54.644
"Mel, it's gonna be okay.

1974
01:25:54.644 --> 01:25:55.730
Like you've got this girl.

1975
01:25:55.730 --> 01:25:59.317
Like, it's lift your head up, you can handle this."

1976
01:25:59.317 --> 01:26:02.254
I don't know what came over me, Rich.

1977
01:26:02.254 --> 01:26:03.671
This is pathetic.

1978
01:26:05.030 --> 01:26:07.147
But standing there in my underwear,

1979
01:26:07.147 --> 01:26:11.407
in front of my bathroom sink, I raised my hand

1980
01:26:11.407 --> 01:26:13.824
and high-fived my reflection.

1981
01:26:14.711 --> 01:26:19.688
And I cracked a smile cause it's so fucking corny.

1982
01:26:19.688 --> 01:26:23.673
I even thought of that guy Stuart Smalley from the SNL skit.

1983
01:26:23.673 --> 01:26:27.984
Just remember that I'm nice, I'm kind, people like me,

1984
01:26:27.984 --> 01:26:29.638
went on with my day.

1985
01:26:29.638 --> 01:26:30.888
That was it.

1986
01:26:30.888 --> 01:26:32.178
Snapped a photo though?

1987
01:26:32.178 --> 01:26:33.011
No, not that one.

1988
01:26:33.011 --> 01:26:34.219
Oh, not that one.

1989
01:26:34.219 --> 01:26:35.145
Not the first time.

1990
01:26:35.145 --> 01:26:36.726
And then I kept doing it.

1991
01:26:36.726 --> 01:26:40.540
I did it probably for a week or two.

1992
01:26:40.540 --> 01:26:42.108
And here's the weird thing about it,

1993
01:26:42.108 --> 01:26:45.678
I started when I woke up, after doing this high five

1994
01:26:45.678 --> 01:26:48.034
your own reflection in the mirror thing,

1995
01:26:48.034 --> 01:26:51.379
I actually started to feel like I was looking forward to it.

1996
01:26:51.379 --> 01:26:52.625
And here's why.

1997
01:26:52.625 --> 01:26:55.094
You know, I've spent a lifetime just like you standing

1998
01:26:55.094 --> 01:26:56.217
in front of the mirror.

1999
01:26:56.217 --> 01:26:58.960
And what I realize now is that when I'm standing in front

2000
01:26:58.960 --> 01:27:03.960
of a mirror, I'm either critiquing or picking myself apart

2001
01:27:04.068 --> 01:27:06.803
or I'm ignoring myself.

2002
01:27:06.803 --> 01:27:09.611
And when you start to high-five your own reflection,

2003
01:27:09.611 --> 01:27:14.603
it starts to build a partnership within you with yourself.

2004
01:27:14.603 --> 01:27:18.157
When you walk into the bathroom and you see your reflection

2005
01:27:18.157 --> 01:27:20.881
and you've been greeting it, it's like seeing

2006
01:27:20.881 --> 01:27:22.337
another person.

2007
01:27:22.337 --> 01:27:23.790
It's the strangest thing.

2008
01:27:23.790 --> 01:27:27.028
You start to realize how often you fucking ignore

2009
01:27:27.028 --> 01:27:28.986
or destroy yourself when you see yourself

2010
01:27:28.986 --> 01:27:30.782
or beat yourself up.

2011
01:27:30.782 --> 01:27:33.725
And here's what's also crazy.

2012
01:27:33.725 --> 01:27:37.248
You have a lifetime, and this is where the science gets

2013
01:27:37.248 --> 01:27:39.667
wacky and I'm gonna hit you with so much science

2014
01:27:39.667 --> 01:27:41.958
because this stuff is so cool.

2015
01:27:41.958 --> 01:27:45.308
You have a lifetime positive association with high-fiving

2016
01:27:45.308 --> 01:27:46.761
other people.

2017
01:27:46.761 --> 01:27:47.594
Sure.

2018
01:27:47.594 --> 01:27:50.915
As a runner, as a racer, you have gotten so many

2019
01:27:50.915 --> 01:27:51.748
high fives, Rich.

2020
01:27:51.748 --> 01:27:53.483
What does a high five say to you when somebody gives you

2021
01:27:53.483 --> 01:27:54.622
one?

2022
01:27:54.622 --> 01:27:55.743
You feel seen.

2023
01:27:55.743 --> 01:27:57.984
You feel appreciated.

2024
01:27:57.984 --> 01:27:59.899
You feel energized by it.

2025
01:27:59.899 --> 01:28:03.108
And it's an exchange of energy.

2026
01:28:03.108 --> 01:28:05.734
It's not the same, and you talk about this in the book,

2027
01:28:05.734 --> 01:28:08.423
it's not the same as like self-talk because there's

2028
01:28:08.423 --> 01:28:11.897
a participation involved in it, there's like a communion

2029
01:28:11.897 --> 01:28:12.730
aspect to it.

2030
01:28:12.730 --> 01:28:15.547
Yeah and, you know, if you think about it,

2031
01:28:15.547 --> 01:28:19.660
you're so good at celebrating, seeing and cheering

2032
01:28:19.660 --> 01:28:20.991
for other people in your life.

2033
01:28:20.991 --> 01:28:22.927
You plan birthday parties.

2034
01:28:22.927 --> 01:28:25.391
You reach out to people when you're worried about them.

2035
01:28:25.391 --> 01:28:26.552
You help out colleagues.

2036
01:28:26.552 --> 01:28:28.296
You cheer for your favorite sports teams.

2037
01:28:28.296 --> 01:28:31.075
Your high five people like Rich as they're running races

2038
01:28:31.075 --> 01:28:32.195
past you.

2039
01:28:32.195 --> 01:28:33.975
You buy people's merchandise.

2040
01:28:33.975 --> 01:28:36.846
You do all kinds of stuff for other people,

2041
01:28:36.846 --> 01:28:39.305
but nobody's taught you how to do it for yourself.

2042
01:28:39.305 --> 01:28:43.857
In fact, the reason why it feels fucking weird to high five

2043
01:28:43.857 --> 01:28:46.692
your own reflection is because you've been taught to do

2044
01:28:46.692 --> 01:28:47.984
the opposite.

2045
01:28:47.984 --> 01:28:51.751
Why is the default to just beat ourselves down like that?

2046
01:28:51.751 --> 01:28:55.669
I mean, it is crazy, we would never treat anyone else

2047
01:28:55.669 --> 01:28:58.326
in our lives, especially the people we care about,

2048
01:28:58.326 --> 01:29:01.351
the way that we treat ourselves in terms of the self-talk

2049
01:29:01.351 --> 01:29:04.878
or the narrative or the critique or the, you know,

2050
01:29:04.878 --> 01:29:08.378
the kind of harshness with which we, you know,

2051
01:29:08.378 --> 01:29:12.171
judge our appearances, our behavior, the way we, you know,

2052
01:29:12.171 --> 01:29:14.525
think back on things that we said the other day

2053
01:29:14.525 --> 01:29:17.751
and just are horrified by our own selves.

2054
01:29:17.751 --> 01:29:20.327
And it's, I don't know if it's everybody.

2055
01:29:20.327 --> 01:29:21.160
Everybody.

2056
01:29:21.160 --> 01:29:21.993
Most people.

2057
01:29:21.993 --> 01:29:22.826
Except for Buddhists.

2058
01:29:22.826 --> 01:29:23.782
I mean, I think that they're like,

2059
01:29:23.782 --> 01:29:26.188
like if you're a big practicing Buddhist, that's a monk.

2060
01:29:26.188 --> 01:29:27.021
Right.

2061
01:29:27.021 --> 01:29:27.854
That's like just.

2062
01:29:27.854 --> 01:29:30.410
Why can't the default be the good things though?

2063
01:29:30.410 --> 01:29:31.243
Well.

2064
01:29:31.243 --> 01:29:32.076
You know, why is it?

2065
01:29:32.076 --> 01:29:32.909
You know why?

2066
01:29:32.909 --> 01:29:34.150
There's cognitive bias.

2067
01:29:34.150 --> 01:29:36.500
There's a bias towards negativity.

2068
01:29:36.500 --> 01:29:38.743
And it's a protection mechanism that's a default

2069
01:29:38.743 --> 01:29:39.576
from evolution.

2070
01:29:39.576 --> 01:29:41.854
That if you remember the bad shit, you're more likely

2071
01:29:41.854 --> 01:29:43.552
to spot it when it happens in the future.

2072
01:29:43.552 --> 01:29:44.385
So you can avoid it.

2073
01:29:44.385 --> 01:29:46.334
And here's where I think it begins.

2074
01:29:46.334 --> 01:29:50.654
I believe my theory is that it begins two places,

2075
01:29:50.654 --> 01:29:54.154
either you or they could be both actually,

2076
01:29:55.281 --> 01:29:58.720
you either learned the pattern of beating yourself up

2077
01:29:58.720 --> 01:30:01.857
because you had parents or caregivers that were hard

2078
01:30:01.857 --> 01:30:03.056
on themselves or hard on you.

2079
01:30:03.056 --> 01:30:05.538
And so as a child you absorbed that pattern

2080
01:30:05.538 --> 01:30:07.615
and you now repeat it and you don't even realize it.

2081
01:30:07.615 --> 01:30:09.514
So those moments you're like, "Oh my God, I sound just like

2082
01:30:09.514 --> 01:30:10.831
my dad or my mom."

2083
01:30:10.831 --> 01:30:13.694
That is an example of a pattern that you've absorbed.

2084
01:30:13.694 --> 01:30:17.636
So particularly for women, we've watched our mothers

2085
01:30:17.636 --> 01:30:19.234
be critical about their appearance.

2086
01:30:19.234 --> 01:30:21.752
We've watched our mother's ignore and criticize themselves

2087
01:30:21.752 --> 01:30:22.585
in the mirror.

2088
01:30:22.585 --> 01:30:26.028
And so, we learn that from our caregivers.

2089
01:30:26.028 --> 01:30:27.604
So that's one place.

2090
01:30:27.604 --> 01:30:30.377
The second place that we learn it is when the drive

2091
01:30:30.377 --> 01:30:32.548
in your life becomes fitting in.

2092
01:30:32.548 --> 01:30:36.371
Fitting into groups in elementary, middle high school,

2093
01:30:36.371 --> 01:30:38.308
college, your neighborhood.

2094
01:30:38.308 --> 01:30:41.323
That feels safe when you fit in.

2095
01:30:41.323 --> 01:30:45.053
When you feel like you don't belong, you immediately go

2096
01:30:45.053 --> 01:30:46.748
into a protection mechanism.

2097
01:30:46.748 --> 01:30:49.607
And I believe a lot of the negative self-talk

2098
01:30:49.607 --> 01:30:53.454
is a sorting hat type of mentality that we do to ourselves

2099
01:30:53.454 --> 01:30:55.071
going, " I can't be with those people.

2100
01:30:55.071 --> 01:30:56.197
I can't be with those people.

2101
01:30:56.197 --> 01:30:57.612
It's safe to be with those people."

2102
01:30:57.612 --> 01:31:00.429
And you start to see yourself and the world around you

2103
01:31:00.429 --> 01:31:03.452
as places where you belong and places where you don't.

2104
01:31:03.452 --> 01:31:06.935
And part of the criticism, as fucked up as it sounds,

2105
01:31:06.935 --> 01:31:10.376
that we engage in all the time is don't be too big,

2106
01:31:10.376 --> 01:31:13.156
don't be too loud, don't show yourself too much,

2107
01:31:13.156 --> 01:31:15.103
don't have blue hair, don't do this,

2108
01:31:15.103 --> 01:31:16.756
other people won't like you.

2109
01:31:16.756 --> 01:31:20.596
It starts as a way to protect yourself from being rejected

2110
01:31:20.596 --> 01:31:24.244
but the truth is, you develop a habit of fucking rejecting

2111
01:31:24.244 --> 01:31:25.077
yourself.

2112
01:31:25.077 --> 01:31:25.910
Right.

2113
01:31:25.910 --> 01:31:27.892
Meanwhile, you're further divorcing yourself

2114
01:31:27.892 --> 01:31:30.189
from who you truly are, because you're not giving yourself

2115
01:31:30.189 --> 01:31:33.092
permission to be yourself.

2116
01:31:33.092 --> 01:31:37.865
That gets sublimated in favor of fitting in and, you know,

2117
01:31:37.865 --> 01:31:41.016
accommodating other people and acclimating your behavior

2118
01:31:41.016 --> 01:31:43.216
around what will be approved of.

2119
01:31:43.216 --> 01:31:44.208
Yes.

2120
01:31:44.208 --> 01:31:49.208
So for me, you know, I have clearly a lifetime of beating

2121
01:31:49.615 --> 01:31:52.586
myself up and tearing myself down and regretting decisions

2122
01:31:52.586 --> 01:31:53.630
that I made.

2123
01:31:53.630 --> 01:31:55.629
And in the middle of stumbling through life,

2124
01:31:55.629 --> 01:31:57.001
instead of being like, "It's gonna be okay,

2125
01:31:57.001 --> 01:31:57.834
it's gonna be okay."

2126
01:31:57.834 --> 01:31:59.718
And being like, "You really fucked up now, Mel."

2127
01:31:59.718 --> 01:32:00.674
How does that help?

2128
01:32:00.674 --> 01:32:01.507
Right.

2129
01:32:01.507 --> 01:32:04.869
How does criticizing and being hard on yourself help?

2130
01:32:04.869 --> 01:32:07.445
You know, what's interesting is I even think of somebody

2131
01:32:07.445 --> 01:32:08.333
like David Goggins, right?

2132
01:32:08.333 --> 01:32:09.694
Everybody looks at him, you can't hurt me.

2133
01:32:09.694 --> 01:32:12.399
Tough, tough, tough, tough, mentally tough.

2134
01:32:12.399 --> 01:32:16.649
His single biggest mindset trick is the cookie jar.

2135
01:32:17.600 --> 01:32:20.949
It's reaching into your mind to find a positive thing

2136
01:32:20.949 --> 01:32:23.850
that's worth fighting through the bullshit for.

2137
01:32:23.850 --> 01:32:26.498
It still comes down to encouragement and support

2138
01:32:26.498 --> 01:32:28.450
and something positive.

2139
01:32:28.450 --> 01:32:33.450
And so to me, the coolest thing about the high five is this.

2140
01:32:33.543 --> 01:32:36.130
First of all, I'm demanding that you try it,

2141
01:32:36.130 --> 01:32:38.747
especially if you think it's stupid as hell.

2142
01:32:38.747 --> 01:32:40.631
I want you to stand in front of the mirror.

2143
01:32:40.631 --> 01:32:43.760
Number one, because research out of Harvard says,

2144
01:32:43.760 --> 01:32:47.343
taking a moment of reflection with yourself in the morning

2145
01:32:47.343 --> 01:32:50.671
and setting an intention changes your productivity.

2146
01:32:50.671 --> 01:32:52.281
It changes your neurochemistry.

2147
01:32:52.281 --> 01:32:54.067
It changes how you show up for the day.

2148
01:32:54.067 --> 01:32:55.272
It changes everything.

2149
01:32:55.272 --> 01:32:56.429
That's number one.

2150
01:32:56.429 --> 01:33:00.160
Number two, you have a lifetime of looking at yourself

2151
01:33:00.160 --> 01:33:02.264
in the mirror and seeing what you hate.

2152
01:33:02.264 --> 01:33:03.706
I wanna change that.

2153
01:33:03.706 --> 01:33:07.719
When you raise your hand and high five your own reflection,

2154
01:33:07.719 --> 01:33:11.162
because you have been raising your hand and high-fiving

2155
01:33:11.162 --> 01:33:13.837
other people for your entire life.

2156
01:33:13.837 --> 01:33:16.835
It is already programmed into your subconscious.

2157
01:33:16.835 --> 01:33:18.861
All of that shit that's positive.

2158
01:33:18.861 --> 01:33:19.734
"I believe in you.

2159
01:33:19.734 --> 01:33:20.567
We got this.

2160
01:33:20.567 --> 01:33:21.483
Come on, shake it off.

2161
01:33:21.483 --> 01:33:22.691
Well, let's get going.

2162
01:33:22.691 --> 01:33:23.524
Here we go.

2163
01:33:23.524 --> 01:33:24.540
I see you.

2164
01:33:24.540 --> 01:33:25.373
I hear you.

2165
01:33:25.373 --> 01:33:26.206
I celebrate."

2166
01:33:26.206 --> 01:33:28.018
All that stuff you've been doing for other people,

2167
01:33:28.018 --> 01:33:30.475
when you raise that hand, the subconscious part

2168
01:33:30.475 --> 01:33:34.591
of your brain kicks in and it overrides all of the negative

2169
01:33:34.591 --> 01:33:35.424
stuff you think.

2170
01:33:35.424 --> 01:33:37.794
You cannot stand in front of that mirror, Rich, and go,

2171
01:33:37.794 --> 01:33:39.823
"God, I look fat," and high five yourself.

2172
01:33:39.823 --> 01:33:41.364
It's impossible.

2173
01:33:41.364 --> 01:33:43.028
Your brain can't do it.

2174
01:33:43.028 --> 01:33:43.861
Right.

2175
01:33:43.861 --> 01:33:46.017
You also can't think about work emails.

2176
01:33:46.017 --> 01:33:48.837
You also can't think about what's stressing you out.

2177
01:33:48.837 --> 01:33:51.233
And here's the thing that I also love about it.

2178
01:33:51.233 --> 01:33:53.473
So this is a field of study called Neurobics,

2179
01:33:53.473 --> 01:33:57.958
you're marrying physical movement with a change in thought.

2180
01:33:57.958 --> 01:33:59.197
The other thing that I.

2181
01:33:59.197 --> 01:34:00.786
It's like neurology aerobics.

2182
01:34:00.786 --> 01:34:01.619
It is.

2183
01:34:01.619 --> 01:34:02.452
Basically.

2184
01:34:02.452 --> 01:34:03.285
That's exactly what it is.

2185
01:34:03.285 --> 01:34:04.118
I found that part of the book fascinating.

2186
01:34:04.118 --> 01:34:07.481
Yeah, I mean, I think, look, you're taking this very simple

2187
01:34:07.481 --> 01:34:10.615
action that we all associate with positive things

2188
01:34:10.615 --> 01:34:14.714
that's backed up by, you know, the neurochemistry

2189
01:34:14.714 --> 01:34:19.714
with this mirror work, which has a tradition in psychology

2190
01:34:20.239 --> 01:34:23.651
in the field of like cultivating self-love.

2191
01:34:23.651 --> 01:34:25.947
It's not like, like the idea of looking in the mirror

2192
01:34:25.947 --> 01:34:27.366
and doing the Stuart Smalley thing.

2193
01:34:27.366 --> 01:34:30.711
Like there's science and research behind this

2194
01:34:30.711 --> 01:34:33.991
to cultivate appreciation for the self.

2195
01:34:33.991 --> 01:34:36.311
Yes, and so there's even more here.

2196
01:34:36.311 --> 01:34:39.916
So the other stuff that's super cool about this is that,

2197
01:34:39.916 --> 01:34:42.095
you know, we've all bought into this lie that somehow

2198
01:34:42.095 --> 01:34:44.197
beating yourself up and tough love and being hard

2199
01:34:44.197 --> 01:34:45.924
on yourself Is motivating.

2200
01:34:45.924 --> 01:34:46.916
Wrong.

2201
01:34:46.916 --> 01:34:50.282
All the research shows that it is de-motivating.

2202
01:34:50.282 --> 01:34:52.851
If you're somebody that's stuck, if you're somebody

2203
01:34:52.851 --> 01:34:56.774
that has regrets, if you're somebody that is tired

2204
01:34:56.774 --> 01:35:00.538
of where you are, if you feel like, you know, you just,

2205
01:35:00.538 --> 01:35:02.116
"Why can't I change?"

2206
01:35:02.116 --> 01:35:05.384
Beating yourself up is making it worse.

2207
01:35:05.384 --> 01:35:10.217
You have to learn how to cheer for yourself where you are.

2208
01:35:12.536 --> 01:35:15.316
Because if you're not, you will never find the motivation

2209
01:35:15.316 --> 01:35:16.149
to change.

2210
01:35:16.149 --> 01:35:18.722
So they did this study with kids where they broke kids

2211
01:35:18.722 --> 01:35:20.307
into three different groups, okay?

2212
01:35:20.307 --> 01:35:24.010
And they gave the kids very challenging problems.

2213
01:35:24.010 --> 01:35:27.574
And they wanted to know what was going to be the thing

2214
01:35:27.574 --> 01:35:31.345
that researchers could do to motivate and inspire kids

2215
01:35:31.345 --> 01:35:35.179
to work through a challenging problem and what is gonna be

2216
01:35:35.179 --> 01:35:37.910
the most effective thing that we could do to give somebody

2217
01:35:37.910 --> 01:35:40.133
that boost that you need to really face something

2218
01:35:40.133 --> 01:35:41.166
that's hard.

2219
01:35:41.166 --> 01:35:44.151
So group number one got the old fixed mindset praise

2220
01:35:44.151 --> 01:35:46.350
where it's like, "Hey, you're super smart, Rich.

2221
01:35:46.350 --> 01:35:48.138
Hey Rich, you're really good looking in those glasses

2222
01:35:48.138 --> 01:35:50.614
and I bet they help you make you focus on this problem,

2223
01:35:50.614 --> 01:35:51.909
keep going, buddy."

2224
01:35:51.909 --> 01:35:54.660
The second group got the fixed mindset praise which is,

2225
01:35:54.660 --> 01:35:57.331
"Hey Rich, you're really, really working hard.

2226
01:35:57.331 --> 01:36:00.261
Rich that perseverance is amazing, keep going."

2227
01:36:00.261 --> 01:36:03.423
The third group, they got a simple high five.

2228
01:36:03.423 --> 01:36:06.175
No words, just a high five.

2229
01:36:06.175 --> 01:36:10.096
The group with the simple high five outperformed

2230
01:36:10.096 --> 01:36:13.008
the other two groups and then some.

2231
01:36:13.008 --> 01:36:14.005
Why?
That's so crazy.

2232
01:36:14.005 --> 01:36:14.958
Why?

2233
01:36:14.958 --> 01:36:16.222
I'll tell you why.

2234
01:36:16.222 --> 01:36:21.139
A high five is fulfilling your fundamental emotional needs.

2235
01:36:22.022 --> 01:36:23.481
It's not about the problem.

2236
01:36:23.481 --> 01:36:26.446
When you high-five somebody, including yourself,

2237
01:36:26.446 --> 01:36:29.728
you are affirming that somebody exists.

2238
01:36:29.728 --> 01:36:34.728
You are saying, I see you and you're celebrating with them.

2239
01:36:34.785 --> 01:36:39.785
You know how those things go viral all year long of teachers

2240
01:36:39.977 --> 01:36:42.335
standing outside their classroom doing individual handshakes

2241
01:36:42.335 --> 01:36:43.418
with kids.

2242
01:36:43.418 --> 01:36:44.251
It's the same thing.

2243
01:36:44.251 --> 01:36:47.271
Every one of those kids is being seen as an individual

2244
01:36:47.271 --> 01:36:48.631
and celebrated.

2245
01:36:48.631 --> 01:36:51.959
There's also that study of the NBA teams, right?

2246
01:36:51.959 --> 01:36:55.605
Where they basically looked at how many high fives

2247
01:36:55.605 --> 01:36:56.877
all the teams were.

2248
01:36:56.877 --> 01:36:59.021
Back taps, fist bumps, yeah, touching.

2249
01:36:59.021 --> 01:37:02.225
And the teams that were giving out the most high fives

2250
01:37:02.225 --> 01:37:03.588
were the highest performing teams.

2251
01:37:03.588 --> 01:37:04.921
Yes.
That's crazy.

2252
01:37:04.921 --> 01:37:08.033
The teams that had the least amount of contact like that,

2253
01:37:08.033 --> 01:37:11.403
of fist bumps and high fives and back taps,

2254
01:37:11.403 --> 01:37:12.236
perform the worst.

2255
01:37:12.236 --> 01:37:13.069
Why?

2256
01:37:13.069 --> 01:37:15.874
Because you're building trust with whomever

2257
01:37:15.874 --> 01:37:17.036
you're high-fiving.

2258
01:37:17.036 --> 01:37:18.907
You see when you stand in front of the mirror

2259
01:37:18.907 --> 01:37:21.776
and instead of going, "God, my jowls look like saddlebags

2260
01:37:21.776 --> 01:37:24.045
and I'm fucked today because my to-do list is so long

2261
01:37:24.045 --> 01:37:26.365
and dah, dah, dah, and I shouldn't have had that,

2262
01:37:26.365 --> 01:37:28.251
dah, dah, dah, dah, dah."

2263
01:37:28.251 --> 01:37:31.515
That sets your mind and your day on a certain course.

2264
01:37:31.515 --> 01:37:34.260
When you walk into the bathroom and you habit stack

2265
01:37:34.260 --> 01:37:37.513
this simple high five along with brushing your teeth,

2266
01:37:37.513 --> 01:37:41.083
you're starting your day by fulfilling your most important

2267
01:37:41.083 --> 01:37:41.916
needs.

2268
01:37:41.916 --> 01:37:44.647
You see yourself, you affirm yourself,

2269
01:37:44.647 --> 01:37:45.851
you celebrate yourself.

2270
01:37:45.851 --> 01:37:49.696
And for those of you that feel stuck, part of your problem

2271
01:37:49.696 --> 01:37:52.655
is, you are withholding that shit from yourself

2272
01:37:52.655 --> 01:37:54.805
until you get to the number of the scale,

2273
01:37:54.805 --> 01:37:58.032
until you get that job, until you do the thing

2274
01:37:58.032 --> 01:37:59.036
