And it was a Vermont landscape.

2525
01:51:18.446 --> 01:51:20.194
And I was drawn into it immediately.

2526
01:51:20.194 --> 01:51:23.079
Like I was no longer in that restaurant.

2527
01:51:23.079 --> 01:51:24.392
I was standing in that field.

2528
01:51:24.392 --> 01:51:25.509
I could feel that breeze,

2529
01:51:25.509 --> 01:51:29.092
I'd never ever had an experience like that with a painting.

2530
01:51:29.092 --> 01:51:30.733
Have you ever had an experience like that with art?

2531
01:51:30.733 --> 01:51:33.281
No, I mean, not like that.

2532
01:51:33.281 --> 01:51:34.114
Not like that.

2533
01:51:34.114 --> 01:51:36.602
I mean, yeah, my sense was that this is very atypical

2534
01:51:36.602 --> 01:51:37.435
for you.

2535
01:51:37.435 --> 01:51:38.268
Very.

2536
01:51:38.268 --> 01:51:40.018
So I kind of lost it.

2537
01:51:42.368 --> 01:51:47.114
The restaurant was quiet and then I leaned back

2538
01:51:47.114 --> 01:51:49.072
and suddenly kind of back in the moment, I'm like,

2539
01:51:49.072 --> 01:51:50.905
"I'm gonna own this painting someday."

2540
01:51:50.905 --> 01:51:53.128
It just, that was what was right there.

2541
01:51:53.128 --> 01:51:55.578
And I looked at the price and it said like $3,000.

2542
01:51:55.578 --> 01:51:56.860
I'm like, "Not today."

2543
01:51:56.860 --> 01:51:59.569
And I went back and I sat down and that was it.

2544
01:51:59.569 --> 01:52:01.605
But the painting never left my mind.

2545
01:52:01.605 --> 01:52:04.003
You see there's something called the Zeigarnik effect.

2546
01:52:04.003 --> 01:52:05.212
I always say it wrong.

2547
01:52:05.212 --> 01:52:07.922
But it's like, there's a checklist in your brain

2548
01:52:07.922 --> 01:52:11.051
that when you have a moment where you're like,

2549
01:52:11.051 --> 01:52:12.895
"This is important."

2550
01:52:12.895 --> 01:52:15.146
Your brain puts it on a checklist.

2551
01:52:15.146 --> 01:52:16.430
Yeah, there's like a notch.

2552
01:52:16.430 --> 01:52:18.185
Yes, a notch happens.

2553
01:52:18.185 --> 01:52:20.997
And now your brain, as part of the filter in the brain,

2554
01:52:20.997 --> 01:52:23.528
is always gonna be kind of scanning the subconscious

2555
01:52:23.528 --> 01:52:27.816
to let anything into your mind related to that incident.

2556
01:52:27.816 --> 01:52:30.295
It can be positive by the way, like with the painting,

2557
01:52:30.295 --> 01:52:32.248
or this is how trauma happens too.

2558
01:52:32.248 --> 01:52:34.918
Like you have this high intensity emotional experience

2559
01:52:34.918 --> 01:52:38.038
that notches something in your brain and now your brain

2560
01:52:38.038 --> 01:52:39.998
kicks in to remember.

2561
01:52:39.998 --> 01:52:44.665
So bottom line is, I always thought about that painting.

2562
01:52:46.088 --> 01:52:47.500
Years go by.

2563
01:52:47.500 --> 01:52:48.986
Whenever somebody would say Vermont,

2564
01:52:48.986 --> 01:52:50.885
I'd think of the painting.

2565
01:52:50.885 --> 01:52:52.837
I don't know why, I can't explain.

2566
01:52:52.837 --> 01:52:54.293
I've never wanted a painting before.

2567
01:52:54.293 --> 01:52:55.869
I was not an art student.

2568
01:52:55.869 --> 01:52:56.858
Just going to law school.

2569
01:52:56.858 --> 01:52:58.561
You sound like you're out collecting art.

2570
01:52:58.561 --> 01:53:00.043
No, no.

2571
01:53:00.043 --> 01:53:04.639
And so I, you know, fast forward the story 10 years and my,

2572
01:53:04.639 --> 01:53:08.495
Chris and I are engaged and we're gonna go up to Vermont

2573
01:53:08.495 --> 01:53:09.650
to see the leaves.

2574
01:53:09.650 --> 01:53:11.885
And immediately I'm like, "We gotta go to Simon Pearce.

2575
01:53:11.885 --> 01:53:13.196
I gotta show you the painting."

2576
01:53:13.196 --> 01:53:16.683
So we pull up to the mill and we walk in and right in front,

2577
01:53:16.683 --> 01:53:18.747
there is, boom, a painting by the same artist,

2578
01:53:18.747 --> 01:53:19.580
Gaal Shepherd.

2579
01:53:19.580 --> 01:53:20.828
And I'm like, "Oh my God, it's here."

2580
01:53:20.828 --> 01:53:25.345
And we race all around the place and it's gone.

2581
01:53:25.345 --> 01:53:27.372
And the funny thing was, and this is the way

2582
01:53:27.372 --> 01:53:28.518
your brain works.

2583
01:53:28.518 --> 01:53:30.842
Chris was more disappointed than I was.

2584
01:53:30.842 --> 01:53:32.969
I literally turned to him and said, "It's okay.

2585
01:53:32.969 --> 01:53:34.960
Dude, like it'd be weird if it were still here

2586
01:53:34.960 --> 01:53:35.793
after 10 years.

2587
01:53:35.793 --> 01:53:37.029
I'm gonna track this thing down."

2588
01:53:37.029 --> 01:53:39.733
I actually said to him, "You know what's gonna happen Chris?

2589
01:53:39.733 --> 01:53:42.646
I'm gonna be able to buy this like four years from now.

2590
01:53:42.646 --> 01:53:44.889
It's gonna be hanging in the corporate lobby

2591
01:53:44.889 --> 01:53:46.130
of some fucking building.

2592
01:53:46.130 --> 01:53:47.819
And I'm gonna have to track down the owner

2593
01:53:47.819 --> 01:53:49.676
and pay like quadruple the money they paid,

2594
01:53:49.676 --> 01:53:50.575
but I'm gonna own this damn thing."

2595
01:53:50.575 --> 01:53:51.408
You have a mission now though.

2596
01:53:51.408 --> 01:53:52.469
I have a mission, yes.

2597
01:53:52.469 --> 01:53:56.198
So a couple years pass and it's my birthday.

2598
01:53:56.198 --> 01:53:58.848
I think I was turning 30 and Chris says,

2599
01:53:58.848 --> 01:54:02.537
Chris gets a couple people to give money.

2600
01:54:02.537 --> 01:54:04.373
And he said I could buy anything I wanted with it.

2601
01:54:04.373 --> 01:54:06.279
So I had what? 500 bucks.

2602
01:54:06.279 --> 01:54:08.918
Now I'm pregnant at the time with our first kid.

2603
01:54:08.918 --> 01:54:10.819
I should have bought a crib or stools.

2604
01:54:10.819 --> 01:54:13.355
But for whatever reason, the Zeigarnik effect takes over,

2605
01:54:13.355 --> 01:54:14.885
and I'm like, "The painting."

2606
01:54:14.885 --> 01:54:18.932
So I call the mill and I get this guy on the phone.

2607
01:54:18.932 --> 01:54:22.074
I'm like, "You know, I'd wanna buy a Gaal Shepherd piece."

2608
01:54:22.074 --> 01:54:24.308
And I said, "Could you send me Polaroids?"

2609
01:54:24.308 --> 01:54:25.310
And he's like, "Sure."

2610
01:54:25.310 --> 01:54:26.458
And I tell him the budget and he goes,

2611
01:54:26.458 --> 01:54:30.316
"I'll send you Polaroids of some of her smaller things."

2612
01:54:30.316 --> 01:54:32.611
She's like blowing up in the meantime, right?

2613
01:54:32.611 --> 01:54:35.786
Yeah, she's a very, very successful Vermont landscape

2614
01:54:35.786 --> 01:54:36.698
artist.

2615
01:54:36.698 --> 01:54:41.281
And so I say to him, "You know, by the way there's this

2616
01:54:42.988 --> 01:54:44.387
one painting."

2617
01:54:44.387 --> 01:54:48.004
And I describe the painting I'd seen over 10 years ago.

2618
01:54:48.004 --> 01:54:50.243
And he said, "Well that was way before my time but I bet

2619
01:54:50.243 --> 01:54:51.978
Gaal will know."

2620
01:54:51.978 --> 01:54:54.005
And I'm like, "Gaal? You know Gaal?"

2621
01:54:54.005 --> 01:54:55.384
He's like, "Of course I know Gaal.

2622
01:54:55.384 --> 01:54:58.223
She like lives down the road.

2623
01:54:58.223 --> 01:55:00.056
Here's her number."

2624
01:55:00.056 --> 01:55:02.882
So for two days I paced around the apartment with 500 bucks

2625
01:55:02.882 --> 01:55:04.664
in my pocket driving Chris crazy.

2626
01:55:04.664 --> 01:55:06.655
'Cause I felt like, what am I gonna say to her?

2627
01:55:06.655 --> 01:55:07.857
And I must be a weirdo.

2628
01:55:07.857 --> 01:55:08.912
I've been stalking this lady.

2629
01:55:08.912 --> 01:55:10.149
I've been thinking about her for 10 years.

2630
01:55:10.149 --> 01:55:11.971
I can't afford her painting.

2631
01:55:11.971 --> 01:55:14.073
You know, who am I to buy a painting?

2632
01:55:14.073 --> 01:55:15.905
Like what the hell?

2633
01:55:15.905 --> 01:55:18.148
So finally Chris is like, "Would you fucking call her

2634
01:55:18.148 --> 01:55:19.432
or I'm gonna call her, you're driving me crazy."

2635
01:55:19.432 --> 01:55:23.083
So I call her and I start talking a mile a minute

2636
01:55:23.083 --> 01:55:25.245
and she was amazing.

2637
01:55:25.245 --> 01:55:29.649
And then I said, "By the way, there is this one painting."

2638
01:55:29.649 --> 01:55:31.639
And I go and I explain this painting.

2639
01:55:31.639 --> 01:55:34.070
"There's a stand of Poplar trees down the center

2640
01:55:34.070 --> 01:55:38.024
and a big mountain scape behind it and this beautiful blue

2641
01:55:38.024 --> 01:55:40.928
Vermont sky and geese flying in formation overhead."

2642
01:55:40.928 --> 01:55:45.096
And, I could hear thinking the other end of the line.

2643
01:55:45.096 --> 01:55:49.695
And she said, "You know, Mel, I've done so many large scale

2644
01:55:49.695 --> 01:55:50.926
paintings over the years.

2645
01:55:50.926 --> 01:55:54.745
I'd hate to mistake the one you're talking about.

2646
01:55:54.745 --> 01:55:57.944
But I'll tell you what, what if my husband and I meet you

2647
01:55:57.944 --> 01:56:00.960
and Chris at the mill at Simon Pearce.

2648
01:56:00.960 --> 01:56:03.451
And we'll walk all around and have lunch and I'll tell you

2649
01:56:03.451 --> 01:56:05.202
the stories behind every painting.

2650
01:56:05.202 --> 01:56:06.666
And if you don't see what you like there,

2651
01:56:06.666 --> 01:56:09.192
I'll take you back to my studio, which is a couple miles

2652
01:56:09.192 --> 01:56:10.227
down the river.

2653
01:56:10.227 --> 01:56:11.928
And you can look at everything I'm working on

2654
01:56:11.928 --> 01:56:13.413
and if you don't see anything you like there,

2655
01:56:13.413 --> 01:56:15.810
then you can go through all my slides and see if you could

2656
01:56:15.810 --> 01:56:17.885
find that painting that you saw 10 years ago."

2657
01:56:17.885 --> 01:56:19.260
And I said, "Deal."

2658
01:56:19.260 --> 01:56:20.957
So a month passes.

2659
01:56:20.957 --> 01:56:22.239
We go up to the mill.

2660
01:56:22.239 --> 01:56:25.013
I walk in, we meet each other, she's amazing.

2661
01:56:25.013 --> 01:56:28.496
She's like twice our age, just an incredibly cool lady.

2662
01:56:28.496 --> 01:56:29.825
She's walking around.

2663
01:56:29.825 --> 01:56:32.025
As we're walking around looking at these paintings, Rich,

2664
01:56:32.025 --> 01:56:33.619
I'm like eight months pregnant.

2665
01:56:33.619 --> 01:56:37.736
I'm realizing, "These are 10 times the amount of money

2666
01:56:37.736 --> 01:56:39.181
that I have.

2667
01:56:39.181 --> 01:56:40.848
I can't afford this.

2668
01:56:42.708 --> 01:56:45.778
Like I don't have this kind of money."

2669
01:56:45.778 --> 01:56:50.051
And I'm getting like more and more into that imposter

2670
01:56:50.051 --> 01:56:51.877
syndrome, what am I doing mode.

2671
01:56:51.877 --> 01:56:53.291
"Oh my God, I'm so embarrassed.

2672
01:56:53.291 --> 01:56:54.945
I'm meeting somebody idolize.

2673
01:56:54.945 --> 01:56:56.596
I don't deserve to be here."

2674
01:56:56.596 --> 01:56:58.825
Wasting her time 'cause she thinks she's gonna get

2675
01:56:58.825 --> 01:56:59.790
a payday out of this.

2676
01:56:59.790 --> 01:57:00.623
Correct.

2677
01:57:00.623 --> 01:57:03.772
We sit down and she goes, "Now that you're sitting down,

2678
01:57:03.772 --> 01:57:06.272
I have something to tell you."

2679
01:57:13.782 --> 01:57:17.251
She said, "There's only been two times in my career

2680
01:57:17.251 --> 01:57:22.084
as an artist that I've done two studies of the same piece.

2681
01:57:22.988 --> 01:57:26.221
And your painting is one of them.

2682
01:57:26.221 --> 01:57:27.888
It's one of a pair."

2683
01:57:29.039 --> 01:57:29.996
She knew all along.

2684
01:57:29.996 --> 01:57:31.029
She knew all along.

2685
01:57:31.029 --> 01:57:33.957
She was playing games with you about not knowing

2686
01:57:33.957 --> 01:57:34.790
which painting.

2687
01:57:34.790 --> 01:57:35.623
Yeah.

2688
01:57:35.623 --> 01:57:38.271
And she said, "The sister piece to the one that you saw

2689
01:57:38.271 --> 01:57:41.756
all those years ago is sitting in my studio right now

2690
01:57:41.756 --> 01:57:45.271
where it's been for the past 11 years."

2691
01:57:45.271 --> 01:57:46.569
I start sobbing.

2692
01:57:46.569 --> 01:57:48.975
Everybody's like totally emotional.

2693
01:57:48.975 --> 01:57:50.877
Her husband's like, "You should have seen her

2694
01:57:50.877 --> 01:57:52.370
when she was on the phone with you.

2695
01:57:52.370 --> 01:57:54.132
It was like she saw a ghost."

2696
01:57:54.132 --> 01:57:58.149
So we get in the car and we drive to the mill.

2697
01:57:58.149 --> 01:58:01.332
And when we walk in, there in the center of this massive

2698
01:58:01.332 --> 01:58:06.332
kind of barn-studio space is an easel with a painting,

2699
01:58:06.543 --> 01:58:08.683
taped up with painter's tape on it.

2700
01:58:08.683 --> 01:58:11.683
And it was the sister to my painting.

2701
01:58:11.683 --> 01:58:14.421
There were slight differences, not as much movement

2702
01:58:14.421 --> 01:58:19.421
in the grass, but it was one of the most extraordinary

2703
01:58:19.481 --> 01:58:20.677
experiences of my life.

2704
01:58:20.677 --> 01:58:25.677
It was as if time suspended and I was standing before that

2705
01:58:26.311 --> 01:58:29.728
painting in 1990 saying it would be mine.

2706
01:58:31.306 --> 01:58:35.889
And there I was 11 years later standing in front of it.

2707
01:58:36.980 --> 01:58:39.106
And then I realized, "Oh my God, I can't fucking afford

2708
01:58:39.106 --> 01:58:40.443
this."

2709
01:58:40.443 --> 01:58:42.512
And Chris walks over and he's like, "What's wrong?"

2710
01:58:42.512 --> 01:58:44.360
And I'm like, "Just promise me someday,

2711
01:58:44.360 --> 01:58:47.073
like promise me that, I don't need jewelry.

2712
01:58:47.073 --> 01:58:48.160
I don't need a car.

2713
01:58:48.160 --> 01:58:50.477
Like just promise me, you'll buy me this thing."

2714
01:58:50.477 --> 01:58:52.458
And he kind of leans over and goes,

2715
01:58:52.458 --> 01:58:54.403
"Hey Gaal, how much for the big one?"

2716
01:58:54.403 --> 01:58:59.295
And she said, "Mel can have it for 500 bucks 'cause clearly

2717
01:58:59.295 --> 01:59:04.295
when I was making it 11 years ago, I was making it for her."

2718
01:59:07.055 --> 01:59:10.303
And so it hangs in my kitchen.

2719
01:59:10.303 --> 01:59:12.760
It's on the back of the book.

2720
01:59:12.760 --> 01:59:14.150
This is the gully though, right?

2721
01:59:14.150 --> 01:59:16.536
Yeah, I think so, yeah.

2722
01:59:16.536 --> 01:59:17.376
Yeah.

2723
01:59:17.376 --> 01:59:22.376
And, it's a reminder every day that your mind is designed

2724
01:59:22.650 --> 01:59:25.111
to help you get what you want.

2725
01:59:25.111 --> 01:59:29.571
I never stopped believing that I could make it happen.

2726
01:59:29.571 --> 01:59:33.284
And when you tell your mind what's important to you,

2727
01:59:33.284 --> 01:59:38.034
there is extraordinary science that proves that your mind

2728
01:59:39.181 --> 01:59:43.836
has a live and ever-changing filter, a live network

2729
01:59:43.836 --> 01:59:47.659
that changes how it views the world, what it lets in,

2730
01:59:47.659 --> 01:59:49.523
what it blocks out.

2731
01:59:49.523 --> 01:59:52.684
And if you program your mind correctly,

2732
01:59:52.684 --> 01:59:55.307
and if you're clear about what you wanna create,

2733
01:59:55.307 --> 01:59:58.923
your mind will help you get what you want.

2734
01:59:58.923 --> 02:00:00.416
It's such a beautiful story.

2735
02:00:00.416 --> 02:00:04.246
But I also think it's more than that because there's this

2736
02:00:04.246 --> 02:00:09.246
quantum metaphysical piece to it because it's not just you

2737
02:00:09.780 --> 02:00:13.574
with that notch and the Zeigarnik effect.

2738
02:00:13.574 --> 02:00:15.641
It's also Gaal.

2739
02:00:15.641 --> 02:00:16.474
Yeah.

2740
02:00:16.474 --> 02:00:19.498
And the intention that she put into that painting

2741
02:00:19.498 --> 02:00:21.573
and whatever was going on with her,

2742
02:00:21.573 --> 02:00:25.428
deep down this knowingness that it was meant for you.

2743
02:00:25.428 --> 02:00:29.675
And it took however long it took for those I-beams to swing

2744
02:00:29.675 --> 02:00:33.076
and sync up so that that union could take place.

2745
02:00:33.076 --> 02:00:33.909
Correct.

2746
02:00:33.909 --> 02:00:36.184
But what I make of this, and I'm interested in hearing

2747
02:00:36.184 --> 02:00:39.728
a little bit more of what you think but, like there's just

2748
02:00:39.728 --> 02:00:41.173
more going on.

2749
02:00:41.173 --> 02:00:42.451
Yes, always.

2750
02:00:42.451 --> 02:00:44.566
Than we're able to perceive.

2751
02:00:44.566 --> 02:00:49.138
And I find great comfort in that, especially when I'm stuck

2752
02:00:49.138 --> 02:00:52.830
or when I'm in a place where I can't see the way forward.

2753
02:00:52.830 --> 02:00:56.811
It helps remind me that this difficult situation

2754
02:00:56.811 --> 02:00:59.644
is happening for me and not to me.

2755
02:01:01.291 --> 02:01:03.874
And you know, I've mentioned this a couple times.

2756
02:01:03.874 --> 02:01:07.002
This is another example of that.

2757
02:01:07.002 --> 02:01:10.476
This is another form of high-fiving your mindset.

2758
02:01:10.476 --> 02:01:14.613
And that is, saying to yourself, "There's something amazing

2759
02:01:14.613 --> 02:01:16.067
that's coming."

2760
02:01:16.067 --> 02:01:19.142
Like as long as you stay in this mindset,

2761
02:01:19.142 --> 02:01:21.007
"I'll just keep my head down.

2762
02:01:21.007 --> 02:01:22.997
I'll keep focused on what I want.

2763
02:01:22.997 --> 02:01:27.148
I'll keep chipping away at it because something amazing

2764
02:01:27.148 --> 02:01:28.055
is going to happen.

2765
02:01:28.055 --> 02:01:30.124
This is preparing me for something."

2766
02:01:30.124 --> 02:01:34.198
All those years, I never gave up believing

2767
02:01:34.198 --> 02:01:35.365
that it was possible.

2768
02:01:35.365 --> 02:01:40.365
If you divorce the timeline from something, you can always

2769
02:01:40.412 --> 02:01:43.098
make a case that it's possible.

2770
02:01:43.098 --> 02:01:45.457
And when you stay in that frame of mind,

2771
02:01:45.457 --> 02:01:47.783
it changes how you show up.

2772
02:01:47.783 --> 02:01:51.716
And it also changes the filter in your brain.

2773
